xplicit assumptions; their SCCs describe only that mixed recorded reachability. Reading order and platform decomposition are separate."}},"passages":[{"ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gibbs-comparison","anchor":"gibbs-comparison","selected":true,"text":"\n\n### 11.1.2 Finite-temperature values and exact Gibbs samples\n\nThis comparison samples the exact Gibbs distribution. The QAOA Born law\nin §11.2 is a separate distribution; none of the following bounds identifies\nthe two. For one fixed instance, let $L_n=\\max_\\sigma H_{n,J}(\\sigma)/n$,\n$\\mathcal F_{n,\\beta}=(n\\beta)^{-1}\\log\\sum_\\sigma e^{\\beta H_{n,J}(\\sigma)}$,\nand let $\\mathcal U_{n,\\beta}$ be the mean energy per spin under the\nGibbs law proportional to $e^{\\beta H_{n,J}(\\sigma)}$. Then\n$$\n0\\le\\mathcal F_{n,\\beta}-L_n\\le\\frac{\\log2}{\\beta},\\qquad\n0\\le L_n-\\mathcal U_{n,\\beta}\\le\\frac{\\log2}{\\beta}.\n$$\nThe first is the log-sum bound. The second uses\n$\\mathcal F=\\mathcal U+S/(n\\beta)$, where the Gibbs entropy satisfies\n$0\\le S\\le n\\log2$, together with $\\mathcal U\\le L_n\\le\\mathcal F$.\nThus both disorder averages approach $P_*$ along every joint\n$n\\to\\infty$, $\\beta\\to\\infty$, by the already established\nconvergence of $\\mathbb E L_n$.\n\nThere is also a finite probability estimate. Every configuration has\nGaussian coefficient-vector norm $\\sqrt{(n-1)/(2n^2)}$ after division\nby $n$, and their maximum has the same Lipschitz bound. Gaussian\nconcentration gives, for $n\\ge2$,\n$$\n\\Pr_J(|L_n-\\mathbb E L_n|>s)\n\\le2\\exp\\{-n^2s^2/(n-1)\\}.\n$$\nFor an exact Gibbs draw $\\Sigma^{\\rm G}$, count the at most $2^n$\nconfigurations with deficit at least $s$ and bound the partition sum\nbelow by its maximal term. This gives, for every instance,\n$$\n\\Pr\\{H_{n,J}(\\Sigma^{\\rm G})/n\\le L_n-s\\mid J\\}\n\\le\\min\\{1,e^{n(\\log2-\\beta s)}\\}.\n$$\nThe lower tail below $P_*-s$ is bounded by\n$\\Pr_J(L_n<P_*-s/2)+\\min\\{1,e^{n(\\log2-\\beta s/2)}\\}$;\nthe upper tail is at most the upper tail of $L_n$. Hence an exact\nGibbs sample also approaches $P_*$ in joint probability. These are\nelementary scalar consequences of the Gibbs law, entropy and Gaussian\nconcentration. They supply neither an efficient sampler nor QAOA angles.\n","content_span":{"start_byte":219370,"end_byte":221279,"start_line":4740,"end_line":4781},"block_span":{"start_byte":219337,"end_byte":221313,"start_line":4740,"end_line":4782},"section_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sections/199476","evidence_class":"source_argument","rationale":"Maps this passage to Finite Gibbs values and exact Gibbs samples. The source locations and local guards are retained in these graph records. Elementary finite Gibbs value and sampling bounds are compared with the already established ground-state limit. Exact Gibbs sampling is not an efficient algorithm and is not the Born distribution of QAOA.","claim_ids":["scalar.finite_gibbs_comparison","scalar.finite_maximum_concentration"],"citation_ids":["authored_gibbs_comparison","authored_r8_scalar_finite_maximum_concentration","qaoa_main_theorem","scalar-r2-26-cite-parisi-formula"],"assumption_ids":["assumptions.qaoa_model_and_limits"]}],"correspondence_links":[{"from":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gibbs-comparison","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.finite_gibbs_comparison","kind":"maps_claim","semantic_class":"passage_correspondence","derived":true,"source_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","source_anchor":"gibbs-comparison","source_fi